HINT -- phase 1 (nmap)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

WHAT TO LOOK FOR
────────────────
Companies name their public servers after what the public uses --
the website (www), email (mail), the online store (shop). Those are
supposed to be out there.

Internal machines get different names. Words that mean "staff only,"
"not for customers," or "we forgot this was here":

    internal     private      staging     legacy
    admin        old          backup      test

Exactly one host in the scan wears a name like that. It's a box that
was never meant to face the outside -- and someone left it facing
the outside anyway. That's the back office. That's your target.

HOW TO SUBMIT
─────────────
Type that host's IP or its hostname at the prompt and hit Enter --
for example:

    jenkins-old

HEADS UP
────────
Guess wrong and the Ants' network renews its addresses: every IP
changes. Re-run  nmap 10.4.12.0/24  and read the names again. The
names don't move even when the numbers do.
